27. Capabilities, Human Development and Development as Freedom
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: II | Topic: Broadening of development economics: basic needs, capabilities and human development; growth-development divergence; development as freedom
Introduction
By the 1970s it was clear that growth did not automatically reduce poverty or improve lives. Development economics therefore broadened from income to well-being, basic needs and freedoms.
Basic needs approach
Promoted by the ILO (1976) and Streeten, Stewart, Hicks: development should first meet minimum requirements for food, shelter, clothing, health, education, water and sanitation, plus participation. It prioritises poverty reduction and direct provision over trickle-down. Criticisms: ambiguity of the minimum, little attention to freedom and choice.
Amartya Sen's capability approach
Sen argues that the end of development is to expand what people are able to be and do.
• Functionings: beings and doings (being nourished, being educated, taking part in community life).
• Capabilities: the real freedoms or set of feasible functionings a person can achieve.
• Income and commodities are means, not ends. People differ in their ability to convert resources into functionings because of age, disability, gender and environment (conversion factors).
• Famines occur not only from food shortage but from loss of entitlements (Poverty and Famines, 1981).
Human development
The UNDP's Human Development Reports (from 1990) put people at the centre: development is a process of enlarging people's choices, with long and healthy lives, knowledge and a decent standard of living. It gave practical expression through the HDI.
Growth-development divergence
Growth is necessary but not sufficient. Countries with similar incomes have different human development (e.g., Kerala versus other Indian states; Sri Lanka, Costa Rica versus oil-rich states). Reasons: public spending priorities, income distribution, gender equality and institutions. "Growth-mediated" and "support-led" security (Dreze and Sen) are two routes to development.
Development as freedom
In Development as Freedom (1999), Sen identifies five instrumental freedoms: political freedoms, economic facilities, social opportunities, transparency guarantees and protective security. These reinforce each other and are also constitutive ends. Development means removing unfreedoms (poverty, tyranny, poor economic opportunities, neglect of public facilities, intolerance).
Evaluation
The approach is rich but hard to operationalise: no agreed list of capabilities (Nussbaum proposes ten) and measurement challenges.
Conclusion
Development is a multidimensional process of expanding human freedoms, with income as one important but not sufficient means.